\chapter{Paper Enrichment}
\label{enrich-module}

Paper metadata from different sources varies widely in completeness.
DBLP provides excellent bibliographic data but no abstracts.
Web of Science's Starter API omits abstracts entirely.
Snowballing via citation lookups often returns papers with only a DOI.
A search returning papers without abstracts or titles is nearly useless for
review purposes.

This chapter implements \emph{paper enrichment}: using DOIs to fetch missing
metadata from providers with better coverage.
The key insight is that the same paper often appears in multiple databases.
A paper found in DBLP likely exists in Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex, where we
can retrieve its abstract, title, authors, and PDF URL.
The DOI serves as the universal identifier linking these representations.

The enrichment function is general-purpose: it fills in \emph{any} missing
fields on a paper, making it useful for multiple scenarios:
\begin{description}
\item[Screening enrichment] Papers from DBLP or WOS need abstracts for
  systematic review screening.
\item[Snowball enrichment] Papers from citation lookups may lack titles,
  authors, and abstracts---enrichment resolves these using the DOI.
\item[Display enrichment] On-demand enrichment when viewing paper details in
  the TUI.
\end{description}

We prioritize Semantic Scholar (good coverage, fast API) with OpenAlex as
fallback (broader coverage, always free).

